nt-2f74bc507044" class=3D"d-body-base mb-4"><strong class=3D"font-bold">2. =
Unrestricted access to business flows.</strong></p><p data-testid=3D"text-t=
reatment-0faea5b78777" class=3D"d-body-base mb-4">The risk of these attacks=
 comes from attackers taking advantage of legitimate business flows in mali=
cious ways. Protecting against these attacks can be challenging because the=
 attacks may appear legitimate and aren=E2=80=99t caused by misconfiguratio=
ns. To detect these attacks, deploy behavioral analytics to identify automa=
ted usage patterns=E2=80=94such as accessing multiple functions faster than=
 a human user could=E2=80=94or implement runtime protection that understand=
s and tracks business logic flows.</p><p data-testid=3D"text-treatment-fff0=
b821d336" class=3D"d-body-base mb-4"><strong class=3D"font-bold">3. Injecti=
on attacks.</strong> Thesevulnerabilities result when attackers send malici=
ous data or commands as part of an API request, with the goal of manipulati=
ng how the backend system processes that input. Common injection attacks in=
clude <a href=3D"https://www.f5.com/sql-injection" class=3D"text-primary ho=
ver:text-carbon underline hover:no-underline underline-offset-2 break-words=
 font-semibold">SQL injection</a>, <a href=3D"https://www.f5.com/glossary/c=
ross-site-scripting" class=3D"text-primary hover:text-carbon underline hove=
r:no-underline underline-offset-2 break-words font-semibold">cross-site scr=
ipting</a>, or <a href=3D"https://www.f5.com/glossary/ssrf" class=3D"text-p=
rimary hover:text-carbon underline hover:no-underline underline-offset-2 br=
eak-words font-semibold">server-side request forgery (SSRF)</a>.</p><p data=
-testid=3D"text-treatment-edb033f68321" class=3D"d-body-base mb-4">Injectio=
n attacks pose a significant risk because they can manipulate APIs into pas=
sing malicious input to backend services=E2=80=94such as databases, search =
engines, or operating system commands=E2=80=94which may interpret the input=
 as legitimate instructions. This can result in data breaches, unauthorized=
 access, or unintended execution of functions. To mitigate this risk, it is=
 important to implement robust input validation and sanitization practices,=
 ensuring that APIs only process safe, expected, and properly formatted dat=
a.</p><p data-testid=3D"text-treatment-a2e4580845e4" class=3D"d-body-base m=
b-4"><strong class=3D"font-bold">4. Unrestricted resource consumption. </st=
rong>This occurs when a series of API requests overloads resources such as =
network bandwidth, CPU, memory, or storage. These attacks, also called reso=
urce exhaustion, can result in <a href=3D"https://www.f5.com/glossary/denia=
l-of-service" class=3D"text-primary hover:text-carbon underline hover:no-un=
derline underline-offset-2 break-words font-semibold">denial of service (Do=
S),</a> which degrades the performance or availability of the API or the un=
derlying system, leading to downtime.</p><p data-testid=3D"text-treatment-d=
e731b95634c" class=3D"d-body-base mb-4">These types of attacks can severely=
 impact operations by taking down critical services, or an entire applicati=
on and may generate excessive infrastructure and operational costs=E2=80=94=
especially when API services can be billed on a per-request basis. To mitig=
ate these risks, implement rate limiting to control the volume of API reque=
sts over defined time intervals, enforce maximum size limits on request par=
ameters and file uploads, and establish spending caps for pay-per-use API s=
ervices.</p><p data-testid=3D"text-treatment-003b9fbd38b0" class=3D"d-body-=
base mb-4"><strong class=3D"font-bold">5.&nbsp;Security misconfiguration.</=
strong>Attackers attempt to find unpatched flaws, services running with ins=
ecure default configurations, or unprotected files and directories to gain =
unauthorized access to APIs. These attacks can occur when security controls=
 such as Transport Layer Security (TLS) or cross-origin resource sharing (C=
ORS) policies have been implemented incorrectly=E2=80=94or not at all=E2=80=
=94or unnecessary features have been enabled.</p><p data-testid=3D"text-tre=
atment-fc29383251f3" class=3D"d-body-base mb-4">The risk here lies in attac=
kers using automated tools to identify and exploit common misconfigurations=
, potentially gaining access to services and sensitive data. This risk is a=
mplified as modern architectures have become increasingly decentralized and=
 distributed across multicloud environments. To defend against these threat=
s, organizations should embed security throughout the API development lifec=
ycle and enforce strong security policies, including <a href=3D"https://www=
.f5.com/glossary/ssl-tls-encryption" target=3D"_self" rel=3D"noopener noref=
errer" class=3D"text-primary hover:text-carbon underline hover:no-underline=
 underline-offset-2 break-words font-semibold">TLS encryption</a>. They sho=
uld also utilize automated security testing tools and perform regular API s=
ecurity audits to proactively identify and remediate vulnerabilities.</p><p=
 data-testid=3D"text-treatment-fc77effcf64e" class=3D"d-body-base mb-4"><st=
rong class=3D"font-bold">6. Improper inventory management. </strong>This oc=
curs when an organization lacks full visibility into their API assets or do=
esn=E2=80=99t keep them updated. APIs are subject to changes and updates ov=
er time, but outdated or insecure API versions may remain in production. In=
 addition, older endpoints may be left running unpatched or with weak secur=
ity requirements, increasing the risk of attacks and exploits.</p><p data-t=
estid=3D"text-treatment-8ec00b381373" class=3D"d-body-base mb-4">Lack of pr=
oper API inventory management is risky because hackers can exploit older ve=
rsions of APIs that haven=E2=80=99t been patched or <a href=3D"https://comm=
unity.f5.com/kb/technicalarticles/out-of-the-shadows-api-discovery-and-secu=
rity/303789" target=3D"_self" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" class=3D"text-pri=
mary hover:text-carbon underline hover:no-underline underline-offset-2 brea=
k-words font-semibold">shadow APIs</a> that haven=E2=80=99t been properly s=
ecured. Protecting against these risks requires maintaining an up-to-date i=
nventory of all API endpoints, regularly reviewing and decommissioning unus=
ed APIs, and ensuring all APIs are patched and updated regularly with the p=
roper oversight and security controls in place.</p><p data-testid=3D"text-t=
reatment-1d5e52df0642" class=3D"d-body-base mb-4"><strong class=3D"font-bol=
d">7.&nbsp;Unsafe consumption of APIs. </strong>Developers often implicitly=
 trust the data obtained from third-party APIs, especially those provided b=
y well-established organizations. As a result, developers may apply less st=
ringent security measures, such as input validation, data sanitization, or =
transport layer protection, under the assumption that the data from these A=
PIs is inherently safe. This can introduce significant security vulnerabili=
ties if the external API is compromised or behaves unexpectedly.</p><p data=
-testid=3D"text-treatment-2a4763badf7d" class=3D"d-body-base mb-4">Trusting=
 the security of integrated third-party APIs introduces significant risk. A=
ttackers may identify these dependencies and exploit weak API protections t=
o send malicious requests, potentially resulting in unauthorized URL redire=
cts, eavesdropping, data interception, or access to sensitive information. =
To mitigate these threats, always communicate with third-party services ove=
r encrypted channels, rigorously validate and sanitize all incoming and out=
going data, avoid blindly following URL redirects, and enforce strict limit=
s on the scope and frequency of interactions with external services.</p><h3=
 data-testid=3D"text-treatment-5c843396992e" class=3D"d-sub-section mt-6 mb=
-3">Overcome the challenges to securing your APIs</h3><p data-testid=3D"tex=
t-treatment-a9f64714ee50" class=3D"d-body-base mb-4">Follow these best prac=
tices for implementing strong API security:</p><ul class=3D"d-body-base lis=
t-disc list-outside pl-nudge-lg"><li class=3D"d-body-base mb-nudge-lg pl-2 =
[&amp;_li:first-child]:my-nudge-lg"><strong class=3D"font-bold">Use strong =
access control, authentication, and authorization. </strong>Access control =
mechanisms that combine robust authentication, such as <a href=3D"https://w=
ww.f5.com/company/blog/glossary/oauth" target=3D"_self" rel=3D"noopener nor=
eferrer" class=3D"text-primary hover:text-carbon underline hover:no-underli=
ne underline-offset-2 break-words font-semibold">OAuth</a> or JSON Web Toke=
n (JWT), and fine-grained authorization are critical for preventing unautho=
rized access to sensitive data or functions. These best practices also help=
 enforce the principle of least privilege, where each user, application, or=
 system should have only the minimum access rights and permissions necessar=
y to perform its function, and nothing more.</li><li class=3D"d-body-base m=
b-nudge-lg pl-2 [&amp;_li:first-child]:my-nudge-lg"><strong class=3D"font-b=
old">Validate all client input rigorously. </strong>Check for correct Conte=
nt-Type headers, enforce strict schema validation, and only allow HTTP meth=
ods explicitly defined for each endpoint. This helps ensure that input adhe=
res to schemas or validation rules and prevents content spoofing and method=
 tampering, where attackers attempt to use unsupported methods to exploit l=
ogic flaws or bypass controls.</li><li class=3D"d-body-base mb-nudge-lg pl-=
2 [&amp;_li:first-child]:my-nudge-lg"><strong class=3D"font-bold">Use end-t=
o-end encryption to protect sensitive data at all stages.</strong> Secure d=
ata in transit using TLS and protect data at rest using strong encryption s=
tandards, such as field-level encryption for sensitive fields. Encrypting a=
ll data exchanged between clients and servers prevents man-in-the-middleatt=
acks, eavesdropping, and data tampering, while encrypting data at rest prot=
ects sensitive data stored in databases, logs, or backups, even if the syst=
em is compromised.</li><li class=3D"d-body-base mb-nudge-lg pl-2 [&amp;_li:=
first-child]:my-nudge-lg"><strong class=3D"font-bold">Limit data exposure. =
</strong>Ensure that API responses include only the data necessary for the =
client=E2=80=99s operation, and sanitize error messages to avoid leaking sy=
stem or security details to minimize the risk of information leakage and re=
connaissance by attackers.</li><li class=3D"d-body-base mb-nudge-lg pl-2 [&=
amp;_li:first-child]:my-nudge-lg"><strong class=3D"font-bold">Conduct conti=
nuous security testing. </strong>Integrate automated tools to scan APIs for=
 vulnerabilities during development and deployment, and supplement with reg=
ular security audits and manual reviews. This helps detect and remediate ri=
sks early, keeping APIs secure throughout their lifecycle, limiting exposur=
e of vulnerabilities in production</li><li class=3D"d-body-base mb-nudge-lg=
 pl-2 [&amp;_li:first-child]:my-nudge-lg"><strong class=3D"font-bold">Monit=
or API usage</strong> <strong class=3D"font-bold">continuously.</strong> En=
sure visibility into your API ecosystem by implementing API discovery tools=
 to detect new or unmanaged APIs. Log all API traffic for auditing, anomaly=
 detection, and compliance for real-time threat detection and reporting.</l=
i><li class=3D"d-body-base mb-nudge-lg pl-2 [&amp;_li:first-child]:my-nudge=
-lg"><strong class=3D"font-bold">Implement an API governance policy. </stro=
ng>Define and enforce organization-wide standards for secure, consistent, a=
nd high-quality API development and management. Governance should address a=
uthentication, data protection, lifecycle management, documentation, securi=
ty posture, and compliance, ensuring APIs are secure by design and manageab=
le at scale.</li></ul><p data-testid=3D"text-treatment-53550da9099b" class=
=3D"d-body-base mb-4">For actionable guidelines for securing your APIs, che=
ck out our blog post, <a href=3D"https://www.f5.com/company/blog/api-securi=
